  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1105 PM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nOur weather pattern has changed little over the last few days,\nthat is why we have seen similar weather with thunderstorms each\nnight. The players that have been giving us this weather is an \nupper level ridge centered over the southeast U.S. and an upper\nlevel trough centered over California. On Friday, the upper level\ntrough will slowly begin to push toward the east across the Great\nBasin. This will help push our plume of moisture eastward. The\nthreat for thunderstorms will continue for most of the area on\nFriday, but the threat for heavier rain will shift east of the Rio\nGrande. As the upper level trough pushes into the upper Midwest on\nSaturday, drier air will flow in behind it and that will lower our\nrain chances on Saturday and by Sunday we will have precipitable\nwater values (PW's) well below an inch, so we may be rain free on\nSunday. By Monday another upper level trough builds into southern\nCalifornia and that begins to draw moisture back into our region.\nBut it will be a slow tap of moisture, so Monday is also looking\npretty dry. But by Tuesday, we will again between an upper level\nridge to our east and an upper level trough to our west and the\ncirculations around the two will pull up abundant moisture up out\nof Mexico. Tuesday through Thursday of next week looks very \nrainy. It is still unclear how much instability we will have, but \nif we have some thunderstorms we will see a return to the threat \nof flash flooding, but if we just have a lot of rain showers, it \nwill be cool and damp, but the chances for flooding will be lower.\n\nTaking a quick look at temperatures. For Friday through Monday, we\nwill see temperatures running near or a little above average. But\nthen as the moisture returns, we will see our high temperatures\ndrop below average for the middle of next week. High temperatures\ncould run 3 to 6 degrees below average by next Wednesday.",
